         "DREF Operational Update\nUganda_Floods\nSome of the submerged houses in Bweramule sub county.\nAppeal:\nMDRUG050\nTotal DREF Allocation:\nCHF 479,715\nCrisis Category:\nYellow\nHazard:\nFlood\nGlide Number:\n-\nPeople Affected:\n69,283 people\nPeople Targeted:\n19,098 people\nEvent Onset:\nSlow\nOperation Start Date:\n22-05-2024\nNew Operational End Date:\n30-11-2024\nTotal Operating Timeframe:\n6 months\nReporting Timeframe Start Date:\n-\nReporting Timeframe End Date:\n-\nAdditional Allocation Requested:\n157,941\nTargeted Areas:\nCentral Region, Eastern Region, Western Region\nPage 1 / 19\nDescription of the Event\nMap of Uganda showing flood affected districts\nDate when the trigger was met\n21-08-2024\nWhat happened, where and when?\nIn April 2024, the Eastern Uganda-Elgon region experienced heavy rainfall, as forecasted by the Uganda National Meteorological Authority\n(UNMA). This resulted in significant impacts from episodic floods, hailstorms, and landslides in various areas, including Mbale, Kapchorwa,\nBulambuli, Bukedea, Butaleja, Sironko, Bududa, and Namisindwa. A total of 18,323 people were affected, including thousands of displaced\nfamilies, with 1,129 houses and several crop lands and infrastructures completely destroyed. The rains intensified in the first half of May,\ncausing water levels to rise and rivers such as Manafwa, Lwakhakha, Sironko, Mpologoma, Awoja, Nbuyonga, and Namatala to overflow,\nincreasing the water flow from the slopes of Mount Elgon. Unprecedented flooding occurred, especially between May 7th and 11th,\nexacerbating the impacts experienced throughout April. By mid-May, 39,185 people had\nbeen affected by the floods, compounding the already dire humanitarian situation from April. Further, Ntoroko district witnessed its\never-recorded highest levels of flooding in August whose assessment reveals that 2,355 households comprising of 11,775 people have\nbeen greatly impacted. \nSince April, the Uganda Red Cross (URCS) has deployed all its resources, including prepositioned stocks and support from the ECHO PPP\nand HIP. However, these stocks and in-country assistance were exhausted due to the continuous rains and the scale of the impact.\nAssessments completed on May 17th, 2024, for earlier affected districts and 21st August for Ntoroko district, revealed the extent of the\nflooding and the number of people displaced and in need of assistance, highlighting the limitations of the current efforts and the urgent\nneed to scale up support. Further affected districts requested URCS assistance for the displaced.\nIn response, URCS organized a meeting with in-country partners as various district assessments were received, reflecting on the increased\nneeds and continued flooding. Following the URCS national floods response plan, it was agreed that the resources engaged needed to be\nscaled up and that international support was required to cover the increasing gaps. This formed the basis for URCS's request for\nresources from the DREF to respond which saw an allocation of CHF 321,774 for an implementation period of three (3) months.\nPage 2 / 19\nThe DREF operation is scheduled to conclude on 31st August 2024. However, ongoing flooding in Ntoroko District has significantly\nimpacted the area. According to the latest needs assessment that was conducted by the District Disaster Management committee on the\n7th of August and a further assessment for Ntoroko district completed and shared on 21st August, 11,775 individuals (2,355Households)\nhave been affected, as a result of the increased flooding bringing the total affected to 69,283 people. Based on this, URCS is requesting an\nadditional allocation and extension of implementation timeframe from three months to six months to allow the NS respond to the\nincreasing flooding in Ntoroko district.\nURCS volunteer conducting field assessment\nOrientation of volunteers on data collection prior to conducting\na CASH feasibility assessment in Budaka District\nScope and Scale\nIn total, by August 2024, the floods have affected 69,283 people since April and rendered 10,366 families displaced. This represents: -\n11,775 people affected in August 2024 in Ntoroko district, 57,508 people affected in April and May 2024 in Budaka, Bukedea, Bulambuli,\nButaleja, Dokolo, Kamuli, Kanungu, Kasese, Katakwi, Kayunga, Kumi, Mbale and Sironko districts; 1,129 houses assessed as totally\ndestroyed by severe floods, hailstorms and landslides. Worst impact being reported following heavy rains of 7th to 11 May and August\n2024 which led to significant floods and riverine floods in 14 districts.\nReports from Joint Assessments, District Disaster Management Committees and Community-Based Disaster Risk Reduction Groups\nindicated that 1,810 people in Sironko District were struck by a severe hailstorm on 23/04/2024, leaving 905 of them homeless. This event\nalso damaged six water facilities and one school, while causing substantial harm to crop, livelihoods, roads, and sanitation. Furthermore,\na landslide on the hilly slopes of Sironko on the same day affected 2,045 people, leaving 190 homeless and damaging 188 houses\ncompletely and 211 partially in Nobongo Sub County. This catastrophe had far-reaching negative implications for schools, water source\nfacilities, livelihoods, and crops.\nOn April 3rd, floods hit Bulambuli's  Bunambutye sub-county, affecting 1,630 people, resulting in six hospitalizations, 170 evacuations, and\nthe destruction of 172 houses, with 154 partially damaged. The impact on water facilities, health, and education was substantial.\nIn Namisindwa, Bumwoni sub-county, a hailstorm on April 2nd affected 825 people, with three people injured. As a result, 46 houses were\ncompletely destroyed while 104 others were partially damaged. This calamity, coupled with floods, inflicted severe damage on schools\nand crops, significantly affecting community well-being. Similarly, a flood in Butaleja district, Bukedi Sub County, on April 3rd affected\n8,055 people, resulting in 166 serious injuries, six missing persons, and destroyed 150 houses completely and 108 others partially. This\nflooding also significantly affected healthcare facilities, water sources, schools, and crops, exacerbating sanitation issues and livelihood\nchallenges. Additionally, a landslide in Bududa district, Buwali sub-county, affected 106 people and led to the complete destruction of 24\nhouses, leaving 106 homeless, along with massive crop damage. In Mbale District, Bukasakya, and Bungokho, heavy rainfall induced a\nhailstorm, impacting 2,439 people, injuring nine, completely destroying 82 houses, and partially damaging 51. This left 109 people\nhomeless and worsened sanitation due to the destruction of hygiene facilities like latrines. Moreover, floods struck Bookies sub-county,\nSironko District, on April 16th, impacting 2,385 people, injuring five, leaving 1530 homeless, and devastating 206 houses completely and\n171 partially. This event had a significant negative impact on livelihoods, water facilities, crops, and road infrastructure, as well as\nsanitation facilities. Following heavy rainfall, a rise in the water level on River Awoja caused a flood in Kolir Sub County, Bukedea district,\naffecting 878 people, completely destroying 72 houses, and partially damaging 57. This resulted in the destruction of three healthcare\nfacilities, 38 water facilities, and crops, contributing to increased community vulnerability.\nOverall, the heavy rainfall in April 2024 wreaked havoc across the Eastern Uganda-Elgon region, highlighting the urgent need for\nhumanitarian response to support the vulnerable communities.\nPage 3 / 19\nThe flooding worsened on first half of May, especially between 7th to May 11th affecting cumulatively 58,508 people (8,011 households) in\n14 districts in Uganda as per multi stakeholder assessment findings. The humanitarian efforts deployed by partners in country over the\nApril events turned short compared to the increase of humanitarian needs following the May floods events. 3,080 Families were displaced\nand rendered homeless due to complete destruction or partial damage of their houses. 31% of the people displaced were estimated to be\nchildren while 18% are elderly. \nThe recent floods in Ntoroko District that have severely impacted 2,355 households as of 21st August 2024, have affected a population of\n11,775 individuals, including 4,981 males, 6,794 females, and 2,725 children. The floods have displaced 2,172 people and caused\nwidespread destruction across multiple sub-counties. In Bweramule Sub-County, 714 households were affected, with 327 people\ndisplaced and significant damage to 679 households. Butungama Sub-County saw 578 households affected, with 250 people displaced and\n450 households partially destroyed. Rwebisengo Sub-County experienced damage to 148 households, displacing 106 people, while\nKanara Sub-County was the most severely impacted, with 1,618 households affected and 1,423 fully destroyed. Kanara Town Council also\nfaced significant damage, with 125 households affected and 66 people displaced. Overall, 1,705 households were fully destroyed,\n1,498were partially destroyed, and 1,865 latrines and 71 water sources were destroyed, highlighting the urgent need for more\nhumanitarian assistance in the district.\nSummary of Changes\nAre you changing the timeframe of the operation\nYes\nAre you changing the operational strategy\nNo\nAre you changing the target population of the operation\nYes\nAre you changing the geographical location\nNo\nAre you making changes to the budget\nYes\nIs this a request for a second allocation\nYes\nHas the forecasted event materialize?\nYes\nPlease explain the summary of changes and justification:\nUganda Red Cross Society (URCS) requests an extension of the current floods DREF for a period of three (3) months taking the\noperation to six (6) months. The extension seeks to raise the current budget from CHF 321,774 to CHF 479,715 raising the target\npopulation from 12,144 to 19,098 people. This request is necessitated by the increased flooding affecting additional districts as a result\nof intensified rains in the month of August 2024 whose details are shared through an assessment report dated 21st August, causing\nwater levels to rise and rivers such as Manafwa, Lwakhakha, Sironko, Mpologoma, Awoja, Nbuyonga, and Namatala to overflow,\nincreasing the water flow from the slopes of Mount Elgon. As a result, unprecedented flooding occurred, in Ntoroko district prompting\nthe district authorities to declare the flooding as a district emergency and called for national government and partners intervention\nthrough a letter dated 20th August 2024. The other districts targeted under this DREF continued to record increased rainfall especially\nbetween May 7th and 11th, exacerbating the flood impacts experienced throughout April when the current DREF was raised.  By mid-\nMay, 57,508 people had been affected by the floods, compounding the already dire humanitarian situation from April. With the latest\nflooding in Ntoroko district with 2,355 households (11,775 people), those affected by the flooding has risen to 10,366 households\ncomprising of 69,283 people. \nIn response to the severe flooding in Ntoroko District, significant adjustments are required to both the timeline and interventions to\naddress the unique needs of the affected communities. The immediate response will tackle urgent issues, such as providing emergency\nwater supplies and sanitation services, given the extensive submersion of 71 water sources and destruction of 1,865 latrines.\nRecognizing the challenge of evacuating due to the importance of livestock in local livelihoods, the response will include targeted\nshelter interventions and multipurpose cash assistance to support families in meeting their needs while keeping their livestock. Health\ninterventions should be enhanced to mitigate disease risks, with a focus on hygiene promotion and the distribution of health kits.\nCommunity Engagement and Accountability (CEA) kiosks should be established to facilitate communication and address the specific\nconcerns of the community, particularly around evacuation challenges. These tailored interventions will meet the immediate needs of\nthe flood-affected population.\nPage 4 / 19\nCurrent National Society Actions\nStart date of National Society actions\n02-04-2024\nHygiene promotion and sensitisation of\nbeneficiaries in Bukedea District\nCBHFA training in Mbale city\nBeneficiary registration exercise for CVA\nShelter, Housing And Settlements\nThrough the ECHO-PPP emergency response budget line, URCS activated the program\nteam to support the needs of persons affected by hailstorms, landslides and floods in\nKisoro, Buikwe, Bunyangabu and Kasese districts. Through the ECHO PPP, URCS\ndistributed 120 EHIs in Buikwe, 100 EHIs and 2 shelter tents in Kisoro, 200 EHIs in Kasese\nand 97 EHIs, 2 shelter tents (200 people capacity) in Bunyangabu. \nUnder the ECHO HIP OXFAM/CRS/URCS consortium, URCS and partners has in this\nresponse supported various interventions in Butalejja, Namisindwa and Bulambuli\nfollowing landslide and flood occurrences; Jointly, the consortium distributed 400 NFI\nkits in Sironko 200, Namisindwa 100 and Bulambuli 100NFIs. And supported 612\nHouseholds with multipurpose cash transfer. This has led to the depletion of both the\nprepositioned stocks and contingency funds under the project.\nThe crisis modifier wasn't requested because it has already been requested (under\nprocessing) for the recent population movement crisis.\nMulti Purpose Cash\nIn May 2024, following the devastating floods that severely impacted livelihoods in\nNtoroko District, the Uganda Red Cross Society (URCS) provided support to the most\nvulnerable households through Multipurpose Cash Assistance under the ECHO PPP\nproject.\nHealth\nURCS deployed its Red Cross Action Teams (RCATs) to support search and rescue efforts\nwhile also activating and deploying three (03) Ambulances and Emergency Medical\nTechnicians/ health staff to support evacuation and referral of injured persons allowing\nthem to receive timely medical care.\nCoordination\nURCS, through its Branch Managers, remains actively engaged in District Disaster\nManagement Committee meetings to ensure a coordinated response to the flooding in\naffected districts including Ntoroko. The Branch Manager provides valuable input and\nupdates during these meetings, which are then documented and shared with the\nRegional Operations Manager. This ongoing communication helps to align local and\nregional response efforts, ensuring that the needs of the affected communities are\naddressed effectively and efficiently\nNational Society Readiness\nThe Uganda Red Cross Society has a well-established capacity to respond to flood\nemergencies, gained through extensive experience over the years from responding to\nfloods. At the onset of the disaster, URCS activated its Branch Disaster Response Teams\n(Red Cross Action Teams/RCAT) in Mbale, Kapchorwa, Sironko, and Kumi Branches. These\nteams promptly supported search and rescue efforts and mobilized three ambulances to\ntransport injured individuals to health facilities. As the flooding continued, URCS\nmobilized additional volunteers backed up by its effective National Response Teams\nPage 5 / 19\n(NRTs). To ensure responding teams are fully equipped, URCS provided them with First\nAid kits and dispatched prepositioned Non-food Household Items (HHIs), and other\nassets to provide initial life-saving response actions and meet the basic needs of affected\nfamilies.\nDespite the availability of resources within the country that could have been deployed\nto support the initial needs of the affected population, these resources are currently\nlimited to continue supporting the response.\nThe Uganda Red Cross Society (URCS) has made significant progress in meeting the\nurgent needs of communities affected by the severe flooding during the March-April-\nMay (MAM) season. Key activities included distributing cash to 216 vulnerable\nhouseholds in Budaka District, Community-Based Health and First Aid (CBHFA) training\nfor 20 volunteers from Mbale, Butaleja, Kamuli, and Sironko, and the orientation and\ndeployment of volunteers to promote hygiene, community engagement and\naccountability (CEA), and protection (Protection Gender and Inclusion) in Bukedea,\nKayunga and Dokolo districts where many latrines were reported to have submerged in\nflooded water. Additionally, essential relief supplies such as 410 Non-Food Items (NFI)\nkits, 1080 Menstrual Hygiene Management (MHM) kits, and 535 shelter materials were\nprocured and prepositioned for future responses.\nThe cash distribution in Budaka District has enabled vulnerable households to meet\nessential household needs, while the deployment of volunteers has enhanced local\ncapacities to manage health risks, particularly in Bukedea and Dokolo, where community\nmembers were trained to construct drying racks, temporary latrines, and handwashing\nstations. The prepositioning of essential relief items has strengthened the National\nSociety’s preparedness for future events, especially in anticipation of the SOND season.\nOverall, the Red Cross's efforts during the flood response have been crucial in restoring\ndignity and stability to the flood affected persons, laying a firm foundation for long-term\nrecovery.\nAssessment\nIn collaboration with the government and other agencies, the Uganda Red Cross Society\nfacilitated initial assessments to determine the extent of flood damage, assess the needs\nof affected populations, and plan response interventions to help communities cope and\nrecover from the floods and landslides that devastated their areas. Resources were\ndeployed in each most of the affected districts to collect the information between 2nd to\n17 May 2024. The assessment findings have been used to develop this response plan.\nIn collaboration with the Ntoroko District Disaster Management Committee, the Uganda\nRed Cross Society conducted initial assessments to determine the extent of flood\ndamage, identify the needs of the affected populations, and plan appropriate response\ninterventions to support community recovery. Resources were strategically deployed\nacross the most impacted areas from 7th to 9th August 2024, to gather critical\ninformation on the impacts of the floods.\nIFRC Network Actions Related To The Current Event\nSecretariat\nThe IFRC Juba cluster delegation team is providing technical guidance on health, logistics,\nPlanning Monitoring, Evaluation and Reporting (PMER), and finance and the Disaster\nmanagement delegate is working with URCS in responding. To ensure proper\nmonitoring of this operation and support to URCS, IFRC Disaster Management Delegate\nwill increase monitoring visits throughout the operation. The IFRC is closely monitoring\nthe flood situation in the country and stands ready to mobilize additional surge\npersonnel to support the operation if the need arises. Through the IFRC GO platform\nand using the various channels, URCS ensures that all Movement partners are informed\nabout all disaster/ emergency events and emergency operations being implemented in\nUganda. The ECHO-PPP Project is currently the only active RCRCM response mechanism,\nand it is overstretched.\nParticipating National Societies\nPartner National Societies (PNS) present in the country include the Netherlands Red\nCross, Belgium Red Cross-Flanders, Austrian Red Cross, and the German Red Cross who\nare directly providing technical and financial assistance to URCS’ humanitarian and\nPage 6 / 19\ndevelopment projects targeting beneficiaries in the various parts of the country. The\nECHO-PPP Project Consortium has supported limited response in the affected districts in\nEastern and Western Uganda.\nICRC Actions Related To The Current Event\nThe International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) is physically present in the country working with the URCS to facilitate effective\nEmergency Preparedness & Response (EPR) services, Safer Access Frameworks (SAF), providing Restoration of Family Links (RFL) needs\nof the refugee population settled in Uganda as well as promoting International Humanitarian Law (IHL) and Communications as well as\ndirect delivery of detention visits for Political detainees through its Kampala and sub-delegations. For this DREF operation, the URCS\nwill engage the ICRC for technical support in the areas of Tracing in Emergency services, SAF training for RCATs and promotion of EPR\ncapacity of Uganda Red Cross Society. ICRC has provided 10 reflector jackets as visibility material to support the field Teams in Dokolo\ndistrict under Lira URCS Branch.\nOther Actors Actions Related To The Current Event\nGovernment has requested international\nassistance\nYes\nNational authorities\nThe Ministry of Disaster Preparedness and Relief within the Office of the Prime Minister\n(OPM) is spearheading the coordination of all actors involved in the response to the\nflooding in the affected districts. This includes representatives from the Ministry of\nHealth, the Uganda Police Force (UPF), the Uganda People's Defence Forces (UPDF),\nDistrict Disaster Management Committees (DDMCs), and development partners such as\nthe Uganda Red Cross Society (URCS). On August 12, 2024, OPM allocated and distributed\nrelief items to the most affected sub counties. The relief package included 21,000\nkilograms of maize, 10,450 kilograms of beans, and 500 buckets, with distribution carried\nout by the DDMC to address the immediate needs of the affected communities.\nUN or other actors\nWorld Health Organization has been seen attending disaster management committee\nmeetings. Medical Teams International (MTI) has been distributing mosquito nets and\nsupporting with Surveillance.\nAre there major coordination mechanism in place?\nThe Uganda Red Cross Society is actively involved in both internal and external coordination efforts. On a national level, the Ministry\nof Disaster Preparedness and Refugees, within the Office of the Prime Minister (OPM), has activated the National Emergency\nCoordination and Operations Centre (NECOC). The Uganda Red Cross Society participates in DRR monthly and weekly operational\ncoordination meetings, where forecasts, previous response actions, and updates from the field are received, and coordinated response\nactions are planned.\nAt the field level, the Office of the Prime Minister through the District Disaster Management Committees (DDMCs) and District\nEmergency Coordination and Operations Centres (DEOCs), facilitates information gathering and coordination related to disasters. The\nUganda Red Cross Society has an Emergency Task Force (ETF), headed by the Secretary-General, with Senior Management Team\nMembers and a multi-skilled operational team at both regional and field operational levels.\nURCS's regional operational base is managed from local Branch Offices, which maintain close connections with the Regional DECOC.\nBranch Managers covering Budaka, Bukedea, Bulambuli, Butaleja, Dokolo, Kamuli, Kanungu, Kasese, Katakwi, Kayunga, Kumi, Mbale,\nNtoroko and Sironko actively participate in their respective District Disaster Management Committee (DDMC) meetings, held in the\naffected districts.\nURCS is also maintaining a strong coordination with the active partners that have been supporting the NS from the onset of these\nflooding period. Meetings were held within the movement and the URCS partners through active project to align on the priorities for\nthe floods response in Uganda. On 11th May 2024 URCS organized for in country partners meeting reflecting on the increased needs\nand continued flooding. From this meeting, it was agreed to seek for any additional support, include international support through the\nDREF to cover the increasing gaps.\nPage 7 / 19\nNeeds (Gaps) Identified\nShelter Housing And Settlements\nThe floods have displaced 2,172 people and caused widespread destruction across multiple sub-counties. In Bweramule Sub-County, 714\nhouseholds were affected, with 327 people displaced and significant damage to 679 households. Butungama Sub-County saw 578\nhouseholds affected, with 250 people displaced and 450 households partially destroyed. Rwebisengo Sub-County experienced damage to\n148 households, displacing 106 people, while Kanara Sub-County was the most severely impacted, with 1,618 households affected and\n1,423 fully destroyed. Kanara Town Council also faced significant damage, with 125 households affected and 66 people displaced. Overall,\n1,705 households were fully destroyed, 1,498were partially destroyed, and 1,865 latrines and 71 water sources were destroyed,\nhighlighting the urgent need for more humanitarian assistance in the district. Some of the affected persons are currently seeking shelter\nwith relatives or friends, while others are staying in makeshift internally displaced camps (IDPs) in school buildings and tents provided by\nURCS. However, living in these makeshift accommodations exposes vulnerable individuals, including children, the elderly, and those with\nchronic illnesses, to respiratory infections and vector-borne diseases like malaria. Urgent intervention is needed to provide temporary\nshelter kits to 1705 displaced persons and relocate 19098 affected persons to safer places.\nMulti purpose cash grants\nThe recent flooding in Ntoroko District has severely impacted the local community, which relies heavily on cattle rearing and fishing for its\nlivelihood. The flooding has created significant gaps that necessitate urgent Multipurpose Cash Support. Many residents are unwilling to\nevacuate due to the inability to leave their livestock behind, and the floods have damaged livestock shelters, requiring financial assistance\nfor repair and replacement. Additionally, the fishing community has suffered income loss due to damaged equipment and disrupted\nfishing activities, highlighting the need for support to repair or replace fishing gear. The disruption of local markets has made it difficult\nfor affected households to access essential goods. Multipurpose Cash Support would address these critical gaps by providing affected\nhouseholds with the flexibility to manage their specific needs, support livestock and fishing recovery, and navigate the increased cost of\nliving.\nHealth\nThe needs assessment findings reveal critical health needs among the flood-affected populations in Ntoroko District. Immediate provision\nof safe and affordable drinking water is essential, as the cost of water has risen significantly and 71 water sources have been submerged,\nforcing people to use unsafe alternatives. The destruction of 1,865 latrines poses a severe risk of disease outbreaks due to inadequate\nsanitation and the contamination of open water sources. There is also an urgent need for disease prevention measures and health\nservices to address potential outbreaks. Additionally, the displacement of people and the disruption of schools have highlighted the\nnecessity for psychosocial support to manage stress and mental health issues. Restoring health facilities and services is crucial, given the\nimpact on community infrastructure, including schools that may serve as temporary health centers. Special attention to child health and\nnutrition is required, particularly for the 2,725 children affected, to address potential malnutrition and health issues.\nWater, Sanitation And Hygiene\nThe severe flooding that began on 5th August,2024 has led to significant damage to water facilities and has critically impacted water\nsources, leaving the displaced population highly vulnerable to waterborne diseases. Rapid assessments reveal that 1865 latrines and 71\nwater sources have been submerged across the affected districts. Hygiene and sanitation are pressing concerns, as sanitation\ninfrastructure including family latrines and public sewer lines has been destroyed, resulting in the contamination of open water sources\nand food with faecal matter.\nProtection, Gender And Inclusion\nThe recent floods in Ntoroko have caused significant loss of property, severely disrupting communication networks and water supplies.\nRoad networks, have been destroyed, and essential services such as access to schools, health facilities, and markets have been\ninterrupted. This dire situation has led to widespread desperation, helplessness, and uncertainty among the affected population. It is\nPage 8 / 19\ncrucial to recognize that women, girls, boys, and men are all impacted differently, with varying needs and coping capacities that affect\ntheir resilience in the face of this crisis.\nCommunity Engagement And Accountability\nTo address the unique challenges faced by the people of Ntoroko, URCS will implement a comprehensive intervention tailored to the\ncommunity’s needs. Given the region's reliance on cattle rearing and the reluctance of many residents to evacuate their flooded areas due\nto concerns about leaving their livestock behind, URCS will focus on creating solutions that balance human and animal needs. This will\ninvolve setting up community meetings to discuss livestock care and exploring options for temporary shelters or support for animals.\nClear information about evacuation procedures and livestock support will be disseminated through community radios and public\nannouncements. CEA kiosks will collect and address feedback, ensuring concerns about livestock are handled promptly. Additionally,\nURCS will collaborate with local authorities to develop practical strategies for livestock management during the floods. Transparent\ncommunication will be maintained to build trust and ensure that all affected individuals understand the support available. Ongoing\nmonitoring will help adapt the intervention as needed, ensuring both people and animals receive the assistance they require\nAny identified gaps/limitations in the assessment\nBududa, as well as low-lying areas such as Butaleja, Bukedea, and Mbale. Some villages remain inaccessible by road, isolated by\ninfrastructure damage and flooding, impeding effective assessment efforts. To address this, URCS is collaborating with Uganda Flying Lab\nto deploy Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) or Drones for remote imaging, aiding in assessing the scale of damage and locating missing or\ntrapped individuals.\nConsidering the current assistance deployed and the needs summarized in the uploaded tables, there is a gaps on to be covered. April\nfloods have been mostly covered through in-country partners and URCS country support with ECHO, OXFAM consortium and\nGovernment. The May impact required a scale-up and the current mobilized resources and stocks are not enough to cover the needs.\nThere still a gap of at least 13,000 people in need and most being displaced. The ongoing rains in some areas and the current limit of\naccess also to consider in the gaps analysis.\nOperational Strategy\nOverall objective of the operation\nThe primary aim of this operation is to deliver immediate relief and facilitate recovery to enhance the living conditions of 800 households\ndisplaced to camps, totaling 4,800 people impacted by the floods  with shelter, essential household items and basic needs, and raising\nawareness on health, water and sanitation to 19,098 people (33% of the affected population) across 14 districts: Budaka, Bukedea,\nBulambuli, Butaleja, Dokolo, Kamuli, Kanungu, Kasese, Katakwi, Kayunga, Kumi, Mbale, Ntoroko and Sironko.  The remining 67% of the\naffected population are being covered by the prepositioned stocks and by other responding partners. The operation is planned for six\nmonths.\nOperation strategy rationale\nTo achieve the above objective, Uganda Red Cross Society will continue to support efforts of Red cross Action Teams (RCATs) in\nundertaking search and rescue services, community engagement to procure family shelter kits & essential household items and distribute\nto displaced households, emergency food aid through Cash and Voucher Assistance (CVA) mainly focused on unconditional cash. In\naddition to implementing activities that facilitate improved Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH), community health. As well as ensuring\nthe affected persons live with dignity and offered assistance without causing any harm to themselves and socio-cultural systems. \na) Shelter\nTo address shelter needs, 800 households targeted for essential household items will also receive IFRC shelter kits, which include\ntarpurlins, rope, and tools, enabling them to reconstruct or build temporary shelters. Trained volunteers will guide affected households\nin adopting safer, flood-tolerant shelter designs to enhance resilience. Additionally, 600 households in IDP camps in Kanara Subcounty\nand Town council will receive 1,200 household item kits, which include 1,600 blankets, 1,600 mosquito nets, 800 kitchen sets, 800\ntarpaulins, 1,600 jerry cans, 1,600 bars of soap, 1,600 floor mats, and 800 solar lamps. The distribution will be conducted with dignity,\nensuring no harm to the environment or individuals. Post-distribution monitoring will be carried out three weeks later to evaluate the\neffectiveness and utilization of the items, guiding future adjustments to NFI kit contents.\nb) CVA \nPage 9 / 19\nURCS will implement a Multipurpose Cash and Voucher Assistance (CVA) program to address the immediate food and basic needs of 800\nhouseholds in IDP camps. This CVA support, based on rapid assessment findings, will be unconditional to cover items not included in the\nin-kind support. The cash grant amount will be determined according to the Minimum Expenditure Basket (MEB). District leaders will\nparticipate in the beneficiary selection process to ensure accuracy and fairness. Orientation sessions will engage district disaster\nmanagement committees, enhancing their ownership and oversight. Post-distribution monitoring will assess the effectiveness of the\nassistance and inform future improvements, while ongoing market price monitoring will address inflation risks and adjust the MEB as\nneeded.\nc) Health\nDisplacement to IDP camps has significantly increased the risk of communicable diseases due to overcrowded and unsanitary conditions.\nAlongside these health risks, the stress of displacement has elevated mental health risks for those affected. In response, URCS will orient\nand deploy 10 psychosocial support volunteers. These volunteers will prioritize providing care to 100 individuals, focusing on those most\nimpacted by the trauma of displacement. Their efforts will include offering emotional support, raising awareness about mental health,\nand helping individuals develop coping strategies for the challenges of living in crowded camps. This targeted approach ensures that the\nmost vulnerable receive the necessary care and support during this challenging time.\nd) WASH\nFlood damage has severely compromised water and sanitation facilities, increasing the risk of waterborne diseases such as diarrhoea. The\naffected areas now face inadequate access to clean water, with deteriorating conditions in hosting centers further raising the risk of\nmosquito-borne diseases and cholera. In response, URCS will focus its WASH activities on improving access to safe water and promoting\nbetter hygiene practices. To support this effort, 20 branch-based volunteers will be oriented and deployed for a 15-day period to engage\nin hygiene promotion, Community Engagement and Accountability (CEA), and Protection, Gender, and Inclusion (PGI) initiatives, targeting\n19,098 people.\nTo further address the critical needs of the affected population, URCS will distribute water treatment tablets to 19,086 people. This\noperation will procure 200 boxes of soap and 1680 Menstrual Hygiene Management (MHM) kits will be procured and distributed. The\ndeployment will cover both hardware and software aspects, ensuring comprehensive support for the affected population.\nTargeting Strategy\nWho will be targeted through this operation?\nThis response will target a total of 19,098 people (3,183 households) representing approximately 33% of the affected population and who\nare among the hardest hit by the floods and landslides, residing in flood-prone areas across Budaka, Bukedea, Bulambuli, Butaleja,\nDokolo, Kamuli, Kanungu, Kasese, Katakwi, Kayunga, Kumi, Mbale, Ntoroko and Sironko districts.the district. These communities have\nsuffered extensive losses, including homes, crops, and personal belongings, resulting in the complete disruption of their livelihoods.\nThe remaining 67% of the affected population have been covered with prepositioned ECHO PPP and HIP stocks as well as those covered\nby other responding partners. These communities have suffered extensive losses, including homes, crops, livestock, and personal\nbelongings, resulting in the complete disruption of their livelihoods.\nExplain the selection criteria for the targeted population\nThe Uganda Red Cross Society (URCS) is dedicated to prioritizing assistance for the most vulnerable individuals, including the elderly,\nwomen-led households, households headed by children, persons with disabilities, individuals living with chronic illnesses, and single\nparent households. Collaborating with volunteers and local authorities, the URCS has undertaken a comprehensive process to identify\nand profile these vulnerable households for targeted support. These special groups are more vulnerable as their ability to bounce back\nquickly is limited.\nAdditionally, the URCS aims to provide aid to internally displaced families lacking access to shelter. To ensure fairness and transparency\nin beneficiary selection, rigorous verification processes are implemented at various levels to prevent duplication or exclusion.\nPage 10 / 19\nTotal Targeted Population\nWomen\n8,117\nRural\n-\nGirls (under 18)\n3,200\nUrban\n40%\nMen\n5,681\nPeople with disabilities (estimated)\n1%\nBoys (under 18)\n2,100\nTotal targeted population\n19,098\nRisk and Security Considerations\nPlease indicate about potential operation risk for this operations and mitigation actions\nRisk\nMitigation action\nPossible outbreak of water-borne diseases, including Cholera,\nTyphoid fever as well as upsurge of malaria cases due to\nincreased vector. Given the context, there is a very high risk of\nthe spread of water-borne diseases consisting of faecal-oral\ndiseases, water-related diseases and aquatic diseases. Destroyed\nor flooded water points and latrines increase the lack of access to\ndrinking water in the targeted departments. This exposes the\naffected population to epidemics and oral-faecal diseases,\nincluding cholera.\nURCS will distribute water treatment tablets as well as conduct\nhygiene and health promotion sessions. URCS will review the\noperational strategy based on epidemiological monitoring in\ncoordination with the authorities.\nRisk of intensification of the crisis, extension of flooded areas: As\nthe rainy season persists, there is a likelihood of further increases\nin water levels and the expansion of flooding to additional areas.\nWith rivers remaining uncleared, the risk of flooding spreading to\nall vulnerable sub-counties in the targeted districts is high. The\nUganda National Meteorological Authority (UNMA) has issued\nearly warning advisories for April based on seasonal weather\nforecasts indicating heightened rainfall in Northern and Eastern\nUganda, as well as parts of Western Uganda, from April to May.\nThis forecast predicts potential impacts, including flooding in low-\nlying areas and regions near major water bodies.\nIn response to this ongoing risk, the Uganda Red Cross Society\n(URCS) will need to conduct comprehensive needs assessments\nand maintain continuous monitoring of the evolving situation.\nThis approach will help identify emerging gaps in assistance,\nallowing for timely updates and potential extension of the\nDisaster Relief Emergency Fund (DREF) operation. Such an\nextension may involve transforming the operation into an\nemergency appeal to encompass newly affected districts and sub-\ncounties within the target areas. This expanded response would\naim to address the needs of all 1,500,000 individuals residing in\nflood-prone areas who remain at risk of being affected by the\nongoing flooding crisis.\nRisk of increased SGBV Cases due to the shelter situations within\nthe communities\nURCS in this response will complement other partners in\naddressing shelter needs while offering adequate referrals.\nPlease indicate any security and safety concerns for this operation\nThe security situation in the Mt Elgon sub-region is stable, with low security concerns expected in flood-affected areas. However,\ncontinued heavy rains, as forecasted by UNMA, may hinder humanitarian access due to impassable road networks, particularly in high-\nhill areas like Kapchorwa and Bududa, as well as low-lying areas such as Butaleja, Bukedea, and Mbale. Some villages remain inaccessible\nby road, isolated by infrastructure damage and flooding, impeding effective assessment efforts.\nThe security situation in Ntoroko District, which borders the Democratic Republic of Congo, is currently stable, with minimal security\nconcerns anticipated in the flood-affected areas. However, the increasing water levels of River Semuliki and Lake Albert, pose significant\nchallenges for humanitarian access. The flooding has led to impassable road networks and infrastructure damage, particularly in remote\nand low-lying areas. Some villages are now isolated, which complicates assessment and aid delivery efforts.\nHas the child safeguarding risk analysis assessment been completed?\nPage 11 / 19\nYes\nPlanned Intervention\nShelter Housing And Settlements\nBudget:\nCHF 163,065\nTargeted Persons:\n4,800\nIndicators\nTitle\nTarget\nActual\n# of multi-sectoral needs assessment carried out.\n1\n3\n# of households that received essential household items.\n800\n410\n# of households that received emergency shelter kits.\n800\n535\n# of NDRT deployed.\n8\n4\n% of families who confirmed the provided shelter items contributed to\nimprove their life\n80\n79\nNumber of PDMs conducted\n1\n0\nProgress Towards Outcome\nSignificant progress has been made in the ongoing DREF operations, with 535 shelter kits and 410 essential household items procured\nand prepositioned at the central warehouse, ensuring readiness for distribution to affected communities. To assess the impact of the\nflooding, 3 multi-sectoral assessments were conducted in Bukedea, Katakwi, and Ntoroko districts. Additionally, 4 National Disaster\nResponse Team (NDRT) members, including two warehouse NDRTs, a DREF Lead, and an NDRT driver, were deployed to support the\noperation (1 Female, 3 Male). The impact of the provided shelter items on the affected families is under evaluation, with findings to be\nreported in the upcoming shelter evaluation report. These actions represent a strong advancement in the operational objectives and\nreflect the commitment to effectively responding to the needs of flood-affected communities.\nMulti Purpose Cash\nBudget:\nCHF 46,200\nTargeted Persons:\n4,801\nIndicators\nTitle\nTarget\nActual\n# of households that received multipurpose cash.\n800\n216\n# of PDMs conducted.\n1\n0\nPage 12 / 19\n% of families that confirmed the cash has supported access to basic\nneeds and food.\n90\n0\n% of families that have used the cash to access more than food.\n60\n0\nProgress Towards Outcome\nIn response to the severe flooding that affected villages in Naboa Subcounty during the MAM season, Budaka District was selected for\ntargeted multipurpose cash assistance under the DREF operation. 216 households have received cash support. The activity included four\nkey activities. First, a CASH feasibility assessment was conducted with 400 respondents, including community heads, market actors, and\ngovernment officials, to evaluate the program's feasibility and impact. Second, local leaders were sensitized, and feedback from the\nassessment was shared to ensure transparency and build community support. Third, a selection criterion was developed in collaboration\nwith local leadership to identify and prioritize the most vulnerable beneficiaries. Finally, those who met the criteria were registered to\nreceive cash support. The Post Distribution Monitoring (PDM), yet to be conducted and will assess the effectiveness of this assistance\nHealth\nBudget:\nCHF 31,240\nTargeted Persons:\n19,098\nIndicators\nTitle\nTarget\nActual\n# of people reached with health risks awareness messages.\n12,144\n12,144\n# of volunteers deployed for Health promotion (ECV) and Community-\nbased Surveillance (CBS) approaches\n70\n65\n# of people reached with health promotion/epidemic risk\ncommunication messages\n19,098\n12,144\n# of people reached by psychological first aid.\n200\n113\n# of first aid kits procured.\n100\n9\n# of volunteers trained on CBHFA.\n70\n20\n# of volunteers deployed for PSS\n10\n10\nProgress Towards Outcome\nDuring the DREF operation, 12,149 people (5556 M:6593 F) were reached with health risk awareness and epidemic risk communication\nmessages in Dokolo, Kayunga and Bukedea slightly surpassing the target of 12,144. A total of 65 volunteers (38 M: 27F) were deployed for\nHealth Promotion and Community-Based Surveillance (CBS), close to the target of 70. However, due to budget constraints, only 20 out of\nthe targeted 70 volunteers were trained on Community-Based Health and First Aid (CBHFA), and 9 standards first aid kits were procured\nout of the intended 100. Despite these limitations, the branch-based volunteers in Butaleja, Kamuli, Mbale and Sironko district\nsuccessfully provided psychological first aid to 113 individuals\nWater, Sanitation And Hygiene\nBudget:\nCHF 82,288\nPage 13 / 19\nTargeted Persons:\n19,098\nIndicators\nTitle\nTarget\nActual\n# of water committees' people in management trained.\n30\n0\n# of water tanks procured and installed.\n14\n0\n# of people reached with hygien promotion sessions.\n19,098\n12,149\n# of hygiene kits procured and distributed.\n1,680\n1,080\n# of people who have access to safe water in the community after the\ninstallation of the tank.\n3,600\n0\n# of strips of Chroline Tablets procured\n10,000\n0\n# of boxes of Soap procured\n200\n0\n# of DNRTs (hard ware & Software) deployed\n4\n4\nProgress Towards Outcome\nHygiene promotion sessions successfully reached 12,149 individuals, surpassing the target of 12,144. All 1,080 hygiene kits have been\nprocured and prepositioned at the central warehouse, ensuring they are ready for distribution. The procurement of the 10-cubic meter\nwater tanks is pending delivery to the central warehouse, as a Local Purchase Order (LPO) has been issued to the supplier. Of the 14\ntargeted tanks, only 5 will be procured and installed as needed in flood-affected communities. Instead of training water user committees,\nan assessment of the impact of floods on water sources in Katakwi, initiated in late July 2024, is being conducted to guide future\ninterventions\nProtection, Gender And Inclusion\nBudget:\nCHF 27,750\nTargeted Persons:\n19,098\nIndicators\nTitle\nTarget\nActual\n# of assessments conducted\n1\n1\n# of volunteers oriented on PGI and SGBV\n100\n65\n#of volunteers deployed for RFL activities\n14\n65\n# of referrals done\n10\n0\n# of people reached with SGBV sessions\n19,098\n12,149\nPage 14 / 19\nProgress Towards Outcome\nSignificant progress has been made in the Protection, Gender, and Inclusion (PGI) and Sexual and Gender-Based Violence (SGBV)\ninitiatives within the DREF operations. Prior to field deployment, a total of 65 volunteers were oriented on PGI, SGBV and RFL, enhancing\ntheir capacity to support affected communities, exceeding the initial target of 14. Although no referrals have been received from the\ncommunity and volunteers to date, efforts are ongoing to establish effective referral pathways. Notably, 12,149 people have been reached\nthrough SGBV awareness sessions, far surpassing the initial target of 100, reflecting the successful expansion of outreach efforts.\nCommunity Engagement And Accountability\nBudget:\nCHF 6,750\nTargeted Persons:\n19,098\nIndicators\nTitle\nTarget\nActual\n# of CEA kiosks estblashed.\n14\n14\n# of people reached with RCCE.\n19,098\n12,149\nProgress Towards Outcome\nThe DREF operation successfully established all 14 planned Community Engagement and Accountability (CEA) kiosks across Dokolo,\nBukedea, and Kayunga districts, ensuring comprehensive community support and feedback channels. Additionally, 12,149 individuals\nwere reached with Risk Communication and Community Engagement (RCCE) messages, slightly exceeding the target of 12,144. Volunteers\nincorporated Community Engagement and Accountability (CEA), Risk Communication and Community Engagement (RCCE), Protection\nGender and Inclusion (PGI), and Restoring Family Links (RFL) into their hygiene promotion activities. This comprehensive approach\nensured that the hygiene promotion efforts were not only effective but also addressed broader community needs and concerns,\nenhancing overall engagement and support throughout the affected districts\nSecretariat Services\nBudget:\nCHF 15,750\nTargeted Persons:\n100\nIndicators\nTitle\nTarget\nActual\n# of monitoring missions conducted\n4\n4\n# of financial spot check conducted\n1\n0\nProgress Towards Outcome\nThe delegation has successfully conducted all 4 planned monitoring missions, carried out by the Logistics and PMER teams, ensuring\nthorough oversight of activities and adherence to operational standards. The final monitoring exercise is currently underway jointly with\nthe NS ahead of the lessons learnt workshop on 31st August 2024\nPage 15 / 19\nNational Society Strengthening\nBudget:\nCHF 77,384\nTargeted Persons:\n19,098\nIndicators\nTitle\nTarget\nActual\n# of volunteers insured\n100\n0\n# of visibility materials procured\n100\n235\n# of monitoring missions conducted\n15\n15\n# of lessons learnt sessions conducted\n1\n0\nProgress Towards Outcome\nThe DREF operation successfully completed all 15 planned monitoring missions, overseen by operations teams including branch\nmanagers, the DREF lead, directors, procurement officers, and warehouse officers. These teams ensured effective monitoring of\nprocurement processes, field activities, and training sessions. Additionally, 235 visibility materials, including t-shirts with URCS and IFRC\nlogos, reflector jackets, gumboots, pull-up banners, and capes, were procured and distributed evenly across all branches, surpassing the\ntarget of 100 and significantly enhancing the operation's visibility. However, volunteer insurance has not been conducted due to a small\nbudget allocated for this purpose. A lessons learned workshop is planned for 30th August 2024.\nAbout Support Services\nHow many staff and volunteers will be involved in this operation. Briefly\ndescribe their role.\nUsing in country resources, URCS deployed 100 volunteers, 15 staff in the 14 affected branches and will continue to support these teams\nfor a period of three months once this request is approved.\nIf there is procurement, will it be done by National Society or IFRC?\nLocal procurement will be carried out in accordance with the IFRC and URCS standard procurement procedures. Current procurement\nplans will include procurement of non-food items including health and WASH items. URCS will work closely with known suppliers and\nIFRC Juba Delegation to ensure quality and value for money. A procurement plan will be developed to ensure timely support to the\noperation. URCS has a warehouse capacity if needed.\nHow will this operation be monitored?\nTwo approaches to monitoring implementation and data collection shall be used to measure the progress and effectiveness of the DREF;\nat the community level (effectiveness of action) and headquarter level (the efficiency of the internal processes). While the National Society\nshall ensure bi-monthly monitoring trips from relevant URCS HQ technical staff, the IFRC will conduct monthly field missions by DM and\nfinance delegates. In addition, given the workload relating to procurement for replenishment of HHIs, the Cluster logistics & procurement\nofficer will also conduct field support travel.\nPage 16 / 19\nPlease briefly explain the National Societies communication strategy for this\noperation\nURCS has a directorate of communication and resource mobilization and therefore will oversee the communication needs of the\noperation through field based NDRT for communication.\nPage 17 / 19\nBudget Overview\nClick here to download the budget file\nPage 18 / 19\nContact Information\nFor further information, specifically related to this operation please contact:\nNational Society contact:\nRobert KWESIGA,, Secretary General, rkwesiga@redcrossug.org, +256414258 701\nIFRC Appeal Manager:\nPapa Moussa Tall, head of delegation, papemoussa.tall@ifrc.org, +211912179511\nIFRC Project Manager:\nDaniel Kyalo Mutinda, Delegate, disaster management, Daniel.MUTINDA@ifrc.org, +254725599105\nIFRC focal point for the emergency:\nDaniel Kyalo Mutinda, Delegate, Disaster Management, Daniel.MUTINDA@ifrc.org, +254725599105\nMedia Contact:\nSusan Nzisa Mbalu, Communications Manager, susan.mbalu@ifrc.org, +254733827654\nClick here for the reference\nPage 19 / 19\n",
